      "entryId": "ecc-9-5",
      "file": "bookThreadDetails",
      "field": "principle",
      "text": "The living know that they shall die, but the dead know not anything, neither have they any more a portion in anything done under the sun. Together with Psalm 146:4 (\"his breath goeth forth... in that very day his thoughts perish\"), Scripture reveals that death is an unconscious sleep (nephesh dissolution) awaiting the physical resurrection at Christ's return. First principle: conditional immortality; the lie of the serpent (\"Ye shall not surely die\") is refuted by the harmonious testimony of the entire canon.",
      "density": 3.5357,
      "words": 82,
      "sentences": 5,
      "maxSentenceWords": 26,
      "gate": "PASS",
      "violations": [],
      "context": {
        "citations": []
      }
    },
    {
      "entryId": "ecc-9-10",
      "file": "bookThreadDetails",
      "field": "principle",
      "text": "No work or knowledge in the grave whither thou goest. First principle: the grave's silence is the urgency of diligence — work is for the living day, not the dark hereafter.",
      "density": 1.975,
      "words": 31,
      "sentences": 2,
      "maxSentenceWords": 21,
      "gate": "PASS",
      "violations": [],
      "context": {
